00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
you  looking at the Supreme Court this  morning where justices are hearing  arguments today and a challenge to the  Trump administration's effort to end the  so-called daca program which shields  nearly 700,000 young immigrants from  deportation Carter Evans spoke to former  Homeland Security Secretary Janet  Napolitano who oversaw daca during the  Obama administration  he also talked with a student whose  future hinges on the court's decision I  don't want to be living in in constant  fear not a day goes by that 31 year-old  min-koo Choi doesn't worry about his  immigration status it really dictates  the fate of my life and in my future can  you imagine a life outside the US I  can't it's this is where my life is this  is where my job my my friends my family  is Choi is one of nearly 700,000 daca  recipients nationwide protected from  possible deportation it doesn't grant  you any sort of legal status it's sort  of a temporary immunity so to speak it  just keeps you from getting removed for  the country exactly and allows you to  move about and work and pay taxes  exactly the acronym is deferred action  for childhood arrivals but we call it  Dhaka University of California president  Janet Napolitano implemented the program  in 2012 when she served as Homeland  Security Secretary in the Obama  administration when a young person is  raised in this country our country has  already invested in that young person  they've gone to our schools they're  going to our universities and colleges  they represent a population that our  country should want to have among its  citizenry do you feel like there's  anything you can do  no Choi is a UC student working to  become a pharmacist currently doing part  of his clinical rotation at a Children's  Hospital in central California he came  to the u.s. from South Korea with his  family when he was 15 years old his  parents and younger sister became legal  US residents but during the family's  green card application process  oi became ineligible i aged out you turn  21 exactly his fate is now in the hands  of the Supreme Court which will have to  decide if president Trump's action to  rescind daca is valid when the president  announced his plans in 2017 he said in a  statement I do not favor punishing  children but we must also recognize that  we are a nation of laws the Trump  administration argues President Obama  overstepped his authority when he  created daca by executive action without  consulting Congress I think the  President does get a lot of discretion  to rescind previous executive action  remember that is not a law that he's  were sending it's not even a regulation  Ilya Shapiro is director of  constitutional studies at the Cato  Institute he wrote a brief for the  Supreme Court I'm a supporter of daca of  the dream act of Congress passing  legislation to provide lawful status and  and other benefits to people who were  brought here illegally as children but  the president can't do that by himself  that is just flat-out wrong the  executive has to set priorities and the  executive has to have the authority to  establish programs like dokko the UC  system home to roughly 1700 daca  recipients now plans to present that  argument to the Supreme Court all this  led by a person who set records for the  number of people she deported while  serving under President Obama there are  some who say you are the reason  President Obama was called the DePorter  in chief the numbers are what the  numbers are I think the question is well  who who was in the numbers do you worry  about getting deported I do yes if if  daca is is a cinder then everything will  be stripped away this could be your your  neighbor this could be your friends it  is impacting their lives and my life to  the point where it can destroy and then  erase everything the Supreme Court is  expected to rule by the end of June  around the same time min-koo Choi will  graduate for CBS this morning Carter  Evans Los Angeles

